Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 13:24:33 GMT
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: SOCI: Crime control

Tim Starr writes:
>As for the question whether there's any reason to believe that crime control
>would be more effective than gun control, the answer is yes.  Until I get
>a more specific question, though, I won't elaborate :-).

That was my question.  Here's a more specific one.  To what extent, if at
all, are potential or actual criminals deterred from committing crimes by
the existence of a police force, whether patrolling the streets or pursuing
those who do commit crimes?  How does the influence of the police compare
with "social" measures, such as architecture designed to inhibit criminal
behaviour, and private citizens being allowed effective means of
self-defence?  (NB. "Social", not "socialist".  I'm not talking about
whether it's organised by the government or by individual citizens.)

I primarily have in mind "everyday" crimes like burglary, retail murder,
public disorder, and fraud, rather than spectacles like the WTC and
Lockerbie bombings, which get onto the front pages but are, IMHO, and with
condolences to the bereaved, of considerably less importance than the mass
of lesser crimes.

This is mainly addressed to the current situation in Western countries,
rather than a hypothetical PPL world.  In the latter, so I understand (I
haven't read Benson) there would be no state police force, but instead
private security guards and agencies empowered to coerce those who break
the rules into standing trial.  To what extent any of that can be described
as "a police force" in the present meaning of that term is moot.

