Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 08:59:26 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: HUMOR:  Fast; cheap; correct. . . .

In the aerospace world, this sentiment is expressed as:

Cost, Schedule, Performance:  choose any two

and it is not humor, but a statement of reality.

In pursuing a design solution, there must be some criteria on
which to base decisions.  Principal among them are the three
listed above.  But you can't have everything.  The sentiment
expresses the reality that you can control for some variables,
but you need at least one free variable.  Otherwise, if you
specify all three conditions, you may end up with an impossible
combination.

Take, for example, the current Space Station program (an example
wich I am painfully aware of right now).  The cost was specified,
the schedule was specified, and the performance was specified.
It was an impossible set of constraints, hence the program 'failed'
by incurring a cost overrun.  The cost is what gave since
it is the easiest thing to change.

Changing the performance involves changing specification documents
and design drawings.  Changing the schedule involves modifying the
contracts of all the tiers of subcontractors (hundreds).  Changing
the cost involves changing the NASA budget - one action by Congress
& the administration.

Dani Eder

