Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 12:56:39 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PORN: CP as Cannibalism

WARNING:  Sensitive folks may find the following post grisly and
disgusting.  My tone should make clear that I do not advocate any of the
practices described, however.  In fact, I discuss them in order to better
understand how to stop them.

I've been searching for an analogy that will help me to understand the
problem of child pornography more fully.  I've found that comparing it to
cannibalism reveals some interesting parallels.

Imagine that Dahmler's tastes were shared by more than a few other people,
so that there was a black market for food prepared from the flesh of
murdered humans, just as there is now one for CP.  I take it as given that
we are willing to outlaw both murdering for meat and using children as
actors or models for CP.  (I leave aside the question of at what age we
should say a child is incapable of consenting to such practices; I assume
that there is such an age, however.)  The question is whether we should
outlaw the possession and distribution of cannibal foods and CP.  

I have already discussed the view that regulating the possession and
distribution of CP will help to decrease the incidence of child abuse.  The
same arguments apply to cannibal foods.  

I also noted that there may be a problem with this economic justification
if demand for CP (and, by extension, for cannibal foods) turns out to be
inelastic.  But I think that the provision of adequate legal substitutes
may provide a way around any inelastic demand problems.  Substitutes for CP
would come from cartoons, morphed images of adults, and youngish-looking
actors or models.  Substitutes for cannibal foods would come by way of
artificially flavored meats or vegetable products.

As I've noted, allowing these substitutes creates certain adminstrative
problems.  Regulators with a moralistic mission will cite these problems as
an excuse for banning the substitutes outright.  A more lenient view would
allow the use of a substitute to be used as an affirmative defence to the
possession or distribution of CP/cannibal foods.  Note that this approach
would encourage the rise of public or private licensing of producers of
CP/cannibal food substitutes.  A still more lenient view would require
prosecutors to prove that accused parties are not using substitutes.

I'm not sure that thinking about cannibal foods helps us much in deciding
amongst these regulatory schemes, since cannibal foods are not (as far as I
know) popular enough to require regulation.  But there are some interesting
parallels between CP and cannibal foods, just the same.  First of all, note
that intent plays a big role in how one treats the regulated product. 
Coppertone can portray a bare baby's butt, and parents can change babies'
diapers in public, without facing CP charges because none of these parties
intend to arouse purient interests.  Hospitals, mortuaries, and Alcor can
likewise handle human flesh without raising suspicions that they are
dealing with cannibal foods.  

There is at least one area where the parallel between CP and cannibal foods
breaks down.  The common law of torts has long recognized an exception from
the usual prohibition against cannibalism in cases of necessity, such as
among shipwreck survivors lost at sea.  I can think of no reason to grant a
parallel exemption to lonely pedophiles.  (One might find that the age of
consent tends to be set lower in areas, such as frontiers, where older
women are rare.  But that is a matter of carnal relations, not CP.)

