Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 13:07:39 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: MIND: Think like an Extropian!

In the 1950's, Polya wrote "How To Solve It", in which general
strategies for solving problems were put forward.  The idea was
that if those strategies could be assimilated (translated from
knowledge to skill), problem solving performance would increase.

Now, I'm no psychologist (you get what you pay for), but I have
had the privilege to observe some capable idea-generating
minds at work, and have come up with some general techniques
which could perhaps explain some of the Extropian magic of
creative thought.  Somebody else has probably done a similar
analysis, but So What!!!

Make these techniques yours, and soar to new heights of
Extropian power!  Add to this list and help us all!

THOUGHT HABITS OF THE STARS
===========================

#1:  Reduce a concept to an absurdly simple case and analyze how
     that case is structured.

#2:  Push a concept to its extreme application
  * What is the limit of applying an operator infinitely?
  * Consider the objects that result from repeated transitive application
    of a relation (Example: "Hands have fingers" is boring, but what
    if fingers have fingers that have fingers.... ==> Bush Robot).
  * Consider a world where many things (or everything) had some
    property. (Example: A world with intelligent robots is one thing;
    a world with intelligent EVERYTHING [cocktail napkins, rocks, ...]
    is quite another).

#3:  Given some statement containing the word "some" or "something", 
     replace that word with "every" or "everything" and consider the
     conditions in which that new statement would be true.  This
     was one heuristic that Lenat's AM program used to make new
     conjectures.

#4:  If something seems obviously bad, imagine a circumstance in which
     it's good, or some way to make it good for YOU.

#5:  Whenever some population (of gizmos, people, programs, whatever) is
     measured in some way (a number attached to each individual), always
     explicitly consider the meaning of the best, worst, and average, no
     matter what the original intent was.

#6:  Whatever location currently lacks some attribute possessed by some
     other location, consider the possibility and benefits of modifying
     that location so it can have that attribute.  Always of particular
     interest: cases where such modification is surprisingly easy and/or
     the benefits are surprisingly large.

#7:  When some statement is of the form:
      If X is true and Y is true and ... and Z is true THEN A is true
        * Remove precondition(s) and imagine a world where A still holds.
        * See whether the conditional might actually be bidirectional
	* Figure out what unstated preconditions are actually required

#8:  Take a piece of conventional wisdom or "reasonable projection" and
     come up with a circumstance in which it would not hold.  If that
     circumstance is reasonable, the result is interesting.

#9:  Focus on functional (rather than structural) specifications of
     common objects or processes.  Figure out alternate ways those
     specifications could be met.

#10: ALWAYS ask "Why?" until some seemingly fundamental issue is
     discovered.  Make a list of those.  Consider what would be
     necessary to make them NON-fundamental.

#11: Given some necessary condition, theorize that it is also
     sufficient, and vice versa.

#12: ALWAYS extrapolate an observed trend.

#13: When a new vocabulary word is learned, often it will either be
     a meme new to you, or a convenient expression for a previously
     fuzzy concept.  When that's the case (Example: Antinomy, for those
     who don't know the word), juxtapose it with your favorite memes
     and see what falls out.

#14: Ask:
    What if it was really big?
    What if it was really small?
    What if there were lots of them?
    What if it was really fast?
    What if it was really slow?
    What if it could last forever?
    What if it was really cheap?
    What if it was really expensive?
    What if it was under my control?
    What if ...

#15: If something works a little bit in some environment, consider what
     environment would make it work a lot better (Example: lighter-than
     air tools aren't too useful on Earth, but what about where the
     atmosphere is denser?)

#16: Ask, "How can X be described as market-based," where X is anything.

#17: Ask, "How can X be described as the product of evolution," where
     X is anything.

derek

