Despite my exploration of CP regulation, I remain troubled by the fact that
all of the scenarios I have discussed require regulation in the public
interest rather than suits brought by individual tortfeasors.  So I am
thinking about another analogy, this time comparing possession of the
fruits of child abuse with possession of stolen property.  This
analogy could work in two ways: 

1) Those who knowingly distribute or possess stolen goods can be considered
as coconspirators with the original thief, since they aid and abet the
original tort.  Perhaps those who knowlingly distribute or possess CP
produced through child abuse could likewise be sued by the child (or
his/her representative) for constructive abuse.

2) Even those who innocently distribute or possess stolen goods must cede
title to an original owner who catches up with his or her stolen goods. 
Perhaps an abused child could likewise claim the right to repossess the
intellectual property that results from any abuse he/she has suffered at
the hands of a CP producer. 

Note that in both cases individuals bring civil suits.  I am still
interested in CP regulations, however, because I can imagine them being
incorporated into the laws of a private community.  CP regulation thus need
not be based in criminal law, nor need it require statist enforcement
mechanisms.


