Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 10:36:27 EST
From: pmetzger@snark.shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: MISC: Waco, BATF, Privatization, etc.

> From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
> 
> Craig Presson writes:
> >I
> >support firm justice based on individual responsibility. If gangstas
> >knew that they would be making license plates or whatever until they
> >had earned enough to compensate their victims, there would quickly be
> >fewer violent gangs.
> 
> Is there any reason to expect "crime control" to work any better than "gun
> control"?

Yes, depending on the risk reward ratio involved. Remember that violent
crime at its current levels in the inner city is largely the result of
various high-profit victimless crimes becoming common buisnesses. If
you can earn only a mediocre living mugging people and you stand a good
chance of going to jail, you are likely going to be far less likely to
do it.

Another problem is that with the current plethora of idiotic crimes,
resources are being horribly misallocated. New York City sends armed
inspectors to search for violations of the recycling law and won't respond
if you tell them that your stolen property is sitting in your neighbor's
garage. When they are not busy catching people who fail to sort their
garbage and people engaging in victimless crimes, about 80% of their
resources will be freed up.

This is, of course, just the standard libertarian reform. With PPL,
things get even better.

We would imagine in a market based system where
people paid for protection rather than getting it via the government
we would expect them to patronize companies that directed their resources
into stopping crimes with victims, and that companies could no longer
laugh at a person demanding that the person who smashed their car
window be caught, the way that the NYPD will laugh at you were you to
make this request today. The market is a wonderful thing.

Perry

