Subject: ECON/ALIEN: Invasion of the Self-Owned Organizations

david director friedman writes (after his cute story :-):
>Robin also asked about why such self-owned organizations had not come
>into existence. I think one could make a good case for some
>religions, such as Catholicism and Mormonism, fitting at least
>roughly into his pattern. 

Sure they could be considered self-owned.  But although they do many
things which seem like investments with a long time horizon, my
question is why they don't just grow by long-term financial investing?
With respect to these actions these organization's values (inferred
from actions) seem suspiciously congruent with that of their human
agents.

Garrett Goebel writes:
>With this I must beg to differ.  Sure, designing a small organization
>isn't that tough... but an AI for an ant wouldn't be either.  Besides
>the fact, that in designing an organization it is inherent that you
>must understand and be able to motivate many separate intelligences.
>Designing such an organization that could stand the test of time would
>be pretty damned hard.  

Well it isn't trivial, but all it has to do is just own some things
and sit on them, and not spent much existing otherwise.  For example,
with private money we would expect money to be offered that paid
interest, even market-risk correlated (and hence high) interest.  In
this context, all you'd have to do to create such an organization is
put some money like this in an envelope, with instructions on use,
seal it up, and pay a trustee to hold it till a given date.  If one
couldn't find a trustee organization one expected to live long enough,
(such as passing it on to one's decendants with strict instructions
not to open it till a certain date) then bury it or something, at put
it inside another envelope with more money, telling the finder they
could keep this money as a fee for following the instructions inside
the inner envelope, and threatening them somehow if they don't.

Like with software, what organizations you can easily design depends
greatly on what other organizations you have available to build on.

Dale Worley adds:
>Also, people are getting better and better at attacking
>poorly-designed organizations, and subverting their resources.  A
>permanent organization has no evolutionary capabilities to improve its
>defenses.

Sure, but overall I'd say the growth of our economy and of more
complex organizations attests to our increasing ability to manage
agency problems and build better organizations.  Even if we aren't
there yet, I'll still hazard that we'll have autonomous organizations
before we have autonomous AIs (especially since the likely legal route
to the latter is the former).

Robin Hanson

